N_BINS_help="
Defines how many of the BINS node-size breakdowns appear in the summary report
"

BINS_help="
Defines the node size breakdowns that appear in the summary report.
For example if '1 256 512' were specified, the report would indicate
how many 1-node jobs ran, how many 256-node jobs ran and how many
512-node jobs ran.  BINS should be specified in ascending sequence and
the last node-size should be equal to the total nodes available in
the system
"

ACT_GRP_LEVEL_help="
Defines how many levels of grouping there are in your accounting setup.
For example:  If ACT_GRP_LEVEL=3, and the account name convention 
was to name accounts in the form 'ssspppg',
	where sss is site name,
	ppp is project number and
	g is sub-project/group number/code,
the accounting data will be summarized and reported in 3 levels:
by site, project and group
"

ACT_NAME_SIM_help="
How many characters (left to right) corresponding to each ACT_GRP_LEVEL
In the case of above example, it should be then
ACT_NAME_SIM	3 3 1
"

ACT_GRP_LABEL_help="
Defines the category label of each ACT_GRP_LEVEL which is used in 
accounting reports.
"

N_BATCH_QUEUE_help="
Defines how many batch queues are available
"

BATCH_QUEUE_help="
Defines the name of each of the N_BATCH_QUEUE, delimited by a space
"

N_DEDICATE_QUEUE_help="
Defines how many dedicated queues are available
"

DEDICATE_QUEUE_help="
Defines the name of each of the N_DEDICATE_QUEUE, delimited by a space
"

NODES_help="
The total number of nodes available on the system
"

MAILER_help="
The type of mailer which will be used to mail users and ADMIN in the 
case of accounting problems. (Typical values: /usr/bin/mailx, /bin/mail)
"

ADMIN_help="
When there is some kind of accounting error (overdrawn account), 
the MACD mails a message to the user causing the problem.  If ADMIN is not 
empty, then the MACD will also send a message to the specified system 
administrator.
"

OPERATOR_help="
In case of a detectable system problem, the OPERATOR is notified 
by a broadcast to the operator terminal.  The OPERATOR string should give 
the name of the system operator.
"

SWITCHLOG_help="
Because the MACD logs can get rather long, they are switched periodically.  
SWITCHLOG specifies how often the log files are to be 
changed.  SWITCHLOG takes one of three values:	
		SWITCHLOG daily
		SWITCHLOG weekly
		SWITCHLOG monthly
"

MACDMODE_help="
MACDMODE allows the administrator to turn off monitoring of CPU quotas.  
Allowed values are:
	acctonly	Only provide accounting reports.  
	macwatch	Provide both accounting reports and CPU quotas.
"

ENFORCE_help="
Specifies the desired level of job control when quotas are exceeded.  
	<blank>		do not kill jobs 
	acctkill	kill jobs when account's allocation is exhausted 
	userkill	kill jobs when user's allocation is exhausted 
"

QUEUE_CHARGE_help="
Specifies the charge rate for a particular batch queue. 
"

DEF_QRATE_help="
Specifies the charge rate for interactive jobs or for jobs run in queues 
that are undefined in the NQSTABLE file.
"

UNDERUSE_help="
Specifies the charge rate for underutilized nodes for an NQS job.  
Under-used node-time is defined as the idle node-time when at least one 
application is running in an NQS partition.
"


IDLERATE_help="
Specifies the charge rate for idle node-time accrued by NQS jobs.  Idle 
time is defined as the time when there is no application running in an NQS 
partition that has been assigned to a job.
"


SYNC_INTERVAL_help="
Specifies the desired time interval in minutes that is used for MACD 
synchronization.  At this interval, MACD gets status information from SMD 
and synchronizes the disk database with the internal tables.
"
#############
prompt="
Please specify or validate the MACS scheduling parameters given in the
following prompts.  The values displayed in brackets are the default
values.  To use the defaults, just press the Return key.  If you want
to change the default or when no default is given, enter the desired
value followed by the Return key.  If you want more information on
a parameter, enter a question mark (?).
"
